in powers of <math>t</math> and pick out the coefficient of <math>t^{-1}</math>. There is only a finite number of places of this kind. The theorem is that the sum of these coefficients of <math>t^{-1}</math> is zero. This we express by
{{block center|<math>\left[\text{R}(s,z)\frac{dz}{dt}\right]_{t^{-1}} = 0</math>.}}
The theorem holds for the case <math>n=1</math>, that is, for rational functions of one variable <math>z</math>; in that case, about any finite point we have <math>z-c=t</math>, and about <math>z=\infty</math> we have <math>z^{-1}=t</math>, and therefore <math>dz/dt=-t^{-2}</math>; in that case, then, the theorem is that in any rational function of <math>z</math>,
{{block center|<math>\Sigma\left(\frac{\text{A}_1}{z-a}+\frac{\text{A}_2}{(z-a)^2}+\dots+\frac{\text{A}_m}{(z-a)^m}\right)+\text{P}z^h+\text{Q}z^{h-1}+\dots+\text{R}</math>,}}

readings.c2s3:
the sum <math>\Sigma\text{A}_1</math> of the sum of the residues at the finite poles is equal to the coefficient of <math>1/z</math> in the expansion, in ascending powers of <math>1/z</math>, about <math>z=\infty</math>; an obvious result. In general, if for a finite place of the algebraic construct associated with <math>f(s,z)=0</math>, whose neighbourhood is given by <math>z=c+t^r, s=d+\text{Q}(t)</math>, there be a coefficient of <math>t^{-1}</math> in <math>\text{R}(s,z)dz/dt</math>, this will be <math>r</math> times the coefficient of <math>t^{-r}</math> in <math>\text{R}(s,z)</math> or <math>\text{R}[d+\text{Q}(t),\ c+t^r]</math>, namely will be the coefficient of <math>t^{-r}</math> in the sum of the <math>r</math> series obtainable from <math>\text{R}[d+\text{Q}(t),\ c+t^r]</math> by replacing <math>t</math> by <math>\omega t</math>, where <math>\omega</math> is an <math>r</math>th root of unity; thus the sum of the coefficients of <math>t^{-1}</math> in <math>\text{R}(s, z)dz/dt</math> for all the places which arise for <math>z=c</math>, and the corresponding values of <math>s</math>, is equal to the coefficient of <math>(z-c)^{-1}</math> in <math>\text{R}(s_1,z)+\text{R}(s_2,z)+\dots+\text{R}(s_n,z)</math>, where <math>s_1, \dots s_n</math> are the <math>n</math> values of <math>s</math> for a value of <math>z</math> near to <math>z=c</math>; this latter sum <math>\Sigma\text{R}(s_i,\ z)</math> is, however, a rational function of <math>z</math> only. Similarly, near <math>z=\infty</math>, for a place given by <math>z^{-1}=t^r,\ s=d+\text{Q}(t)</math>, or <math>s^{-1}=\text{Q}(t)</math>, the coefficient of <math>t^{-1}</math> in <math>\text{R}(s,z)dz/dt</math> is equal to <math>-r</math> times the coefficient of <math>t^r</math> in <math>\text{R}[d+\text{Q}(t),\ t^{-r}]</math>, that is equal to the negative coefficient of <math>z^{-1}</math> in the sum of the <math>r</math> series <math>\text{R}[d+\text{Q}(\omega t),\ t^{-r}]</math>, so that, as before, the sum of the coefficients of <math>t^{-1}</math> in <math>\text{R}(s,z)dz/dt</math> at the various places which arise for <math>z=\infty</math> is equal to the negative coefficient of <math>z^{-1}</math> in the same rational function of <math>z</math>, <math>\Sigma\text{R}(s_i, z)</math>. Thus, from the corresponding theorem for rational functions of one variable, the general theorem now being proved is seen to follow.

Apply this theorem now to the rational function of <math>s</math> and <math>z</math>,
{{block center|<math>\frac{1}{\text{R}(s,z)}\frac{d\text{R}(s,z)}{dz}</math>;}}
at a zero of <math>\text{R}(s,z)</math> near which <math>\text{R}(s,z)=t^m\text{H}(t)</math>, we have
{{block center|<math>\frac{1}{\text{R}(s,z)}\frac{d\text{R}(s,z)}{dz}\,\frac{dz}{dt}=\frac{d}{dt}\{\lambda[\text{R}(s,z)]\}</math>,}}
where <math>\lambda</math> denotes the generalized logarithmic function, that is equal to
{{block center|<math>mt^{-1}+</math> power series in <math>t</math>;}}

readings.c2s4:
similarly at a place for which <math>\text{R}(s, z) = t^{-\mu}\text{K}(t)</math>; the theorem

{{block center|<math>\left[\frac{1}{\text{R}(s, z)} \frac{d\text{R}(s, z)}{dz} \frac{dz}{dt}\right]_{t^{-1}} = 0</math>}}

thus gives <math>\Sigma m = \Sigma\mu</math>, or, in words, the total number of zeros of <math>\text{R}(s, z)</math> on the algebraic construct is equal to the total number of its poles. The same is therefore true of the function <math>\text{R}(s, z) - \text{A}</math>, where <math>\text{A}</math> is an arbitrary constant; thus the number in question, being equal to the number of poles of <math>\text{R}(s,z) - \text{A}</math>, is equal also to the number of times that <math>\text{R}(s, z) = \text{A}</math> on the algebraic construct.
